<h2 id="main-content">Main Content</h2>

<p>This article focuses on property wrappers in SwiftUI. These wrappers are all used for data binding as the single source of truth for views. The four approaches have subtle differences in functionality. A summary and comparison will be provided at the end.</p>

<ul>
  <li>State</li>
  <li>Binding</li>
  <li>ObservableObject</li>
  <li>EnvironmentObject</li>
</ul>

<h4 id="1-state">1. @State</h4>

<p>SwiftUI manages the stored property declared as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">state</code>. When the value changes, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">SwiftUI</code> updates the parts of the view hierarchy that depend on that value. Use <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@State</code> as the single source of truth for a given value stored in the view hierarchy.</p>

<p>Although the property modified by <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@State</code> is a stored property, we can perform read and write operations on it.</p>

<p>When passing this property between parent and child views, only value passing is allowed.</p>

<p>You need to prefix the property name with a dollar sign <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">$</code> to access this value, because it is a projected value.</p>

<p>Code:</p>

